Most businesses treat international SEO as a translation exercise. Translate the pages, point a new domain at them, and wait for rankings. It rarely works. As an international SEO consultant, I help UK businesses expand into new markets with the technical architecture, content strategy, and hreflang implementation required to actually rank across multiple countries and languages.

If you are generating revenue domestically and want to replicate that success in the US, Europe, or beyond, this page explains exactly how I approach international SEO and what working together looks like.

## Why international SEO is different from domestic

Domestic SEO operates within a single market. You optimise for one language, one search engine locale, and one set of user expectations. International SEO introduces complexity at every level.

Search engines need explicit signals to understand which version of your content serves which market. Without those signals, Google will guess, and it frequently guesses wrong. Your UK pages cannibalise your US pages. Your German content never surfaces in google.de because Google does not recognise it as the primary version for that market.

Beyond the technical layer, user intent shifts between markets. The same keyword in English can carry different commercial intent in the US versus Australia. A product category that dominates in the UK may not exist in the same form in France. International SEO requires market-level research, not just translation.

This is why a proper [technical SEO audit](/services/technical-seo-audit/) forms the foundation of every international engagement. Without understanding your current technical state, layering on international architecture creates more problems than it solves.

## Expanding from the UK into the US with organic search

**A UK company can grow organically in the US from its existing domain. The usual route is a /us/ subfolder, en-gb and en-us hreflang, US spelling and dollar pricing on the US pages, and US keyword research. Report US results separately from day one. I scope this after a free 20-minute diagnosis.**

The UK and US share a language. That is the main risk. Google can treat your UK and US pages as duplicates. It may show the UK page to American searchers or show neither well. The work below gives each market its own clear version. Ongoing UK to US work sits in an [SEO retainer](/services/seo-retainer/) from £1,500 per month.

### Subfolder, separate domain or subdomain for the US?

| Option | Example | When it fits | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|---|
| Subfolder | example.co.uk/us/ or example.com/us/ | Most UK companies starting in the US | Shares the authority of your main domain; one site to maintain |
| Separate domain (ccTLD or US-only .com) | example.us | A US entity with its own team and budget | Starts with little authority; two sites to build and maintain |
| Subdomain | us.example.com | When the US site must run on a different platform | Often treated more like a separate site; more setup to maintain |

A /us/ subfolder on the .com is usually the simplest start if you already own it and it serves UK visitors. The choice is harder when your main site is a .co.uk. A .co.uk domain is tied to the UK. I weigh that with you before any build.

### Hreflang for en-gb and en-us

Each UK page and its US equivalent should reference each other with hreflang: en-gb for the UK page, en-us for the US page and x-default for the version shown to everyone else. Each page also lists itself. The tags must be reciprocal and point at live, indexable URLs.

Google explains the rules in its [guide to localised versions of a page](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/specialty/international/localized-versions). You can draft the tags with my free [hreflang generator](/tools/hreflang-generator/).

### US spelling, prices and pages

US pages need more than a spelling change from "optimisation" to "optimization". They need US spelling, prices in dollars, US sizes and units, US delivery and returns terms and US contact routes. The pages should read as if written for an American buyer. That buyer compares you with US competitors.

### US-specific keyword research

US searchers often use different words. "Sneakers" against "trainers", "attorney" against "solicitor", "zip code" against "postcode". Search volumes and competitors also differ. I research the US market as its own market. UK keywords are not copied across.

### Local entity signals

Search engines and AI assistants look for signs that a business serves the US. Use a US address and phone number only if they are real: a registered office, a warehouse or a staffed number. A virtual address used only for SEO creates trust problems. Say clearly that you ship to or serve US customers from the UK where you have no US presence.

### Measuring the US separately

Filter the Performance report in Google Search Console by country (United States) and by the /us/ folder. Track US clicks, impressions and enquiries apart from UK figures. A UK headline number can hide a weak US start or a strong one.

### The first 90 days

1. **Weeks 1 to 2: audit and decide.** Check your current site, US demand and the US competitors you will meet. Choose subfolder, ccTLD or subdomain.
2. **Weeks 3 to 4: US keyword map.** Research US terms and map them to the pages the US version needs first.
3. **Weeks 5 to 8: build the core US pages.** Localise your highest-value pages: spelling, dollar pricing, US terms and contact details.
4. **Weeks 5 to 8: hreflang and sitemaps.** Add reciprocal en-gb, en-us and x-default tags. List the US pages in the XML sitemap.
5. **Weeks 9 to 12: measure and extend.** Set up the US country and folder filters in Search Console. Check indexing of the US pages and plan the next set of US pages from the data.

US rankings take time to build and depend on your competitors there. I do not guarantee a position or a date.

## Common international SEO mistakes

I audit international sites regularly and see the same failures across industries. These are the ones that cost the most traffic.

### Wrong domain structure

Choosing between ccTLDs (country-code top-level domains like .co.uk or .de), subdirectories (/en-gb/, /de/), and subdomains (de.example.com) is the most consequential decision in international SEO. Each option has trade-offs around domain authority consolidation, hosting complexity, and geo-targeting precision. Most businesses choose based on what their developer finds easiest to implement rather than what serves their SEO goals.

### Missing or broken hreflang

Hreflang tags tell search engines which page serves which language-country combination. Implementation errors are extraordinarily common. Google's John Mueller has said hreflang is one of the most complex aspects of SEO. I regularly find sites with self-referencing tags missing, x-default not set, non-reciprocal annotations, and hreflang pointing at redirecting URLs. Any of these breaks the entire system.

### Duplicate content across markets

English-speaking markets create a particular problem. If your UK and US pages contain identical content with no hreflang and no meaningful localisation, Google treats them as duplicates. One version gets indexed. The other disappears.

This is where [semantic SEO](/services/semantic-seo/) principles become critical: each market version needs content that genuinely serves its audience, not a copy-paste with the spelling changed.

## My international SEO services

Every international engagement is tailored to the business, but the core services cover the same ground.

### Hreflang architecture and implementation

I design and implement hreflang annotation systems, whether through HTML link elements, HTTP headers, or XML sitemaps, based on your CMS and technical constraints. This includes full auditing of existing implementations, correction of errors, and ongoing monitoring to catch breaks before they cost rankings.

### ccTLD vs subdirectory vs subdomain strategy

I evaluate your current domain setup, authority profile, budget, and target markets to recommend the right structure. For most businesses expanding from the UK, subdirectories offer the best balance of authority consolidation and implementation simplicity. But ccTLDs make sense for brands with strong in-market presence. I give you the recommendation with the data behind it.

### Multi-market keyword research

Keywords do not translate. I conduct native-level keyword research for each target market, identifying the terms your actual customers use, not machine-translated approximations of your UK terms. This research feeds directly into content briefs and on-page optimisation for each market version.

### International content strategy

Ranking in a new market requires more than translating existing pages.

I develop content strategies built on [topical authority](/services/topical-authority/) principles for each target market, identifying content gaps, local competitors, and the specific topics needed to build authority from scratch in a new geography.

### Technical international SEO

Beyond hreflang, international sites face challenges around server location, CDN configuration, currency and pricing markup, localised structured data, XML sitemap segmentation, and Search Console property management across markets. I handle the full technical scope.

